---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { features, providers, useCases } from "../lib/data";
---

<Base title={site.title}>
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="kicker">A guide for non-engineers</p>
    <h1>{site.title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Seven providers rent out computers for AI agents. They look alike on a pricing page and
      behave very differently once your agent is running. This site explains each feature in plain
      language, shows what each provider actually offers, and says who to pick for which situation.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="measure">
    <h2>What a sandbox is</h2>
    <p>
      A sandbox is a computer in a data centre that you rent by the hour, or by the second, and can
      throw away when you are done. It has its own disk, its own network address, and usually its own
      copy of Linux. You do not buy hardware, wait for a delivery, or patch a machine in a spare room.
    </p>
    <p>
      People need one for an AI agent because an agent does things: it runs commands, installs
      packages, edits files, opens a browser. Letting it do that on your laptop means it can break
      your laptop, and it stops working the moment you close the lid. A sandbox gives the agent a
      separate computer that stays awake, keeps its files, and can be rebuilt if it goes wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      The word is also used loosely. Some providers mean a container, which is a process isolated by
      the host's shared kernel. Some mean a virtual machine, which is a whole computer with its own
      kernel. The difference matters when the agent runs untrusted code, and it is one of the things
      the <a href="/features/">feature pages</a> make explicit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to use this site</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>1. Start with your situation</h3>
        <p>
          A personal agent, an AI-built app, team workspaces and browser automation each need
          different things. The <a href="/use-cases/">use cases</a> page names the picks for each.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>2. Check the feature that worries you</h3>
        <p>
          Each <a href="/features/">feature page</a> explains the concept, compares all seven
          providers, lists the questions to ask, and gives situational picks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>3. Read the provider page</h3>
        <p>
          Every <a href="/providers/">provider page</a> collects the details in one place: sizes,
          billing, usability, caveats and sources.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Features, in the order they bite</h2>
    <ul class="index-list">
      {
        features.map((feature) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/features/${feature.id}/`}>{feature.title}</a>
            <p>{feature.standfirst}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      Words that mean different things at different providers — pause, stop, snapshot, fork, credits —
      have their own page: <a href="/same-word-different-meaning/">same word, different meaning</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="callout callout-quiet">
      <p class="callout-title">Before you commit</p>
      <p>
        A trial, a credit, a free plan and a monthly allowance are four different offers, and only one
        of the seven converts itself into a subscription.
        <a href="/features/trying-it-before-you-pay/">See what each provider actually gives you before you pay</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Use cases</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      {
        useCases.map((useCase) => (
          <div class="card">
            <h3>
              <a href={`/use-cases/#${useCase.id}`}>{useCase.title}</a>
            </h3>
            <p>{useCase.standfirst}</p>
            <p class="meta">
              Picks: {useCase.picks.map((p) => p.provider).join(", ")}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The seven providers</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      {
        providers.map((provider) => (
          <div class="card">
            <h3>
              <a href={`/providers/${provider.id}/`}>{provider.name}</a>
            </h3>
            <p>{provider.oneLiner}</p>
            <p class="meta">
              {provider.status === "researched" ? `Checked ${provider.checkedOn}` : "Research pending"}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout-title">What this site is not</p>
    <p>
      It is documentation research, not testing. No account was created and no sandbox was started,
      so every claim about behaviour is something a provider wrote down, not something we measured.
      Prices are list prices on {site.checkedOn} and several changed during October 2026. Where a
      provider publishes nothing, the site says “not published” rather than estimating.
      <a href="/how-we-checked/">How we checked</a> explains the tags and the sources.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
